2026-11-18T19:36:47.9770440Z    image gate: fort_dearborn_massacre_monument_1893 aic_taylor_1893: verdict "differs", 1 difference(s) open: the faces, hair, dress and undress, ornaments and weapons as sculpted: since T-3588 the bodies are the people rigs (hm08, posed to the joints read here), so each figure has a body, hands, feet and a head with the rig's neutral face and short hair, and the warrior and Black Partridge their rigs' breechcloths, Black Partridge his leggings, Mrs. Helm her rig's gown, the surgeon his coat and pantaloons and the child his jacket and trousers, all cast in the one bronze; none of it is yet modelled to the sculpted faces, hair, drapery, ornaments and weapons (T-3589, L-fort-dearborn-monument-3567) [T-3594: Mrs. Helm's drapery modelled since: her skirt falls from the waist in long folds over the knee she sinks on and spreads on the rock, folds deepening to the hem; an overskirt is caught up over her left hip, its hem slanting down across the front to below her right knee; and the cloak hangs off her right forearm in a heavy fold gathered at its foot, in place of the cone and the two members the poses kept.] [T-3594, the weapons and ornaments, read on ICHi-19208 for their outline and on this print for their depth, and hung on the posed bodies: the warrior's pipe tomahawk (a haft through his fist with a short butt above it, the flat blade flaring to the right on its lower half and the bowl opposite), his broad feather off the back of his head to the upper right, and the fringed pouch off his belt at the hip, its end cut into four tails; Black Partridge's wide headband with its knot at the crown, the broad feather rising out of it to the upper left and the thin one streaming back and down to the right, its end hooked; and his musket, a slim barrel, the lock, a narrow wrist with the trigger guard, and the butt to its comb and toe, outside his left thigh. Still to model: the faces, the hair, and the dress of the warrior, Black Partridge, the surgeon and the child] [T-3594, the heads turned as both plates show: the warrior's face bowed to Mrs. Helm's shoulder, Black Partridge's in profile to the warrior, each head turned and nodded on its neck with its centre where it was read; on this picture Black Partridge's profile falls on the plate's. Still to model: the faces themselves, the hair (Black Partridge's falling back behind his head is the plate's and not the render's), and the dress of the warrior, Black Partridge, the surgeon and the child] [T-3594, the dress of Black Partridge and the child: Black Partridge's breechcloth is the plates' short apron off his belt, falling over the tops of his thighs and cut at its foot into a ragged fringe (it ends a quarter of the way down his thigh in this print, as the render's does), where the rig's hung to his knee; and the child wears the smock both plates show, from the shoulders to the middle of his thighs, his arms, shins and feet bare, in place of the rig's jacket, trousers and shoes. Still to model: the faces themselves, the hair (Black Partridge's falling back behind his head is the plate's and not the render's), and the dress of the warrior (the fringed sash at his belt) and the surgeon] [T-3594, the hair: Black Partridge's long hair falls from under the back of his band behind his neck to the back of his left shoulder (this print, x 705-750, y 235-305, puts it further back than ICHi-19208 does: about 0.6 m behind the head's centre against about 0.2; it is built at 0.35 m, within 23 px of this print across), and the warrior's lock hangs from under his band past his cheek, curled at its end (x 465-482, y 275-300, built within 4 px of it). Still to model: the faces themselves, and the dress of the warrior (the fringed sash at his belt) and the surgeon] [T-3594, the warrior's sash: a broad band about 0.12 m deep wrapped round his hips with folds across it, as this print draws it (x 410-470, y 395-428), its foot sloping down to his right hip, the fringed ends flying west out of it (the pouch), and the cloth hanging from it at his right hip with strands at its foot. Through this camera the whole sash falls about 40 px lower on the body than the print's, the two fits' known disagreement on the group's height (its heights are ICHi-19208's), and the hanging cloth reads narrower than the print's bunched fold. Still to model: the faces themselves, and the surgeon's dress] [T-3594, the surgeon's pantaloons: in place of his rig's tight ones, loose heavy cloth on both legs from the hip past the knee to the boot's top, rumpled in creases round and across the leg and gathered wider over the boot, where this print shows the leg he stretches west (x 400-470, y 560-640) creased and thick to the boot; the shin is built about 0.15 m through, the print's reading. The render's group stands about 25 px lower on this print than the plate's (the two fits' known disagreement), so the leg is compared by its width and its creases, not its height. Still to model: the faces themselves] [T-3594, the faces turned: Mrs. Helm's face, which looked straight out to the street over a level head, is turned to the warrior and bowed, three-quarter on and down through this camera as the print draws it (x 625-660, y 300-335), the profile ICHi-19208 shows square-on; the child's face, which looked straight out, is lifted up and to the right after his hands, the chin up and three-quarter on as the print draws it (x 745-780, y 555-585). The render's group stands lower on this print than the plate's (the two fits' known disagreement), so the faces are compared by their turn, not their height. Still to model: the sculpted features of the faces themselves (the brows, noses and mouths the print draws stronger than the rig's neutral face)]
